      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix (Computer Use) Stabilize Codex Computer Use readiness",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nCodex Computer Use readiness could go stale or look healthy when it was not actually usable. OpenClaw agents can run with isolated per-agent `CODEX_HOME` directories, so one agent could keep a stale cached Computer Use plugin after the desktop app updated. Status/setup also reported coarse plugin availability without separately proving installation, MCP exposure, and live `list_apps` behavior.\n\nThe macOS desktop bundle has also moved from `/Applications/Codex.app` to `/Applications/ChatGPT.app`. A managed integration that only probes the legacy bundle can lose both the app-server binary and bundled Computer Use marketplace after the desktop update.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR hardens Codex Computer Use setup so OpenClaw no longer treats “plugin exists somewhere” as sufficient readiness.\n\nIt adds:\n\n- opt-in shared local bundled Computer Use plugin cache reconciliation for per-agent `CODEX_HOME` directories; the default `independent` mode leaves existing per-agent caches unmanaged, while `shared` refreshes a real Codex-discoverable copy and removes stale versions\n- current/legacy macOS desktop compatibility: managed app-server and bundled marketplace discovery prefer `ChatGPT.app`, fall back to `Codex.app`, then retain the existing plugin-local/package binary fallbacks\n- decomposed `/codex computer-use status` results for installation, MCP exposure, and a live `computer-use.list_apps` probe\n- truthful readiness: a failed live probe always reports `ready: false`; startup compatibility is evaluated separately when `strictReadiness` is false, while `strictReadiness: true` fails closed\n- explicit `/codex computer-use install` remains strict and only succeeds after installation, MCP exposure, and the live probe all pass\n- a 60s live-probe timeout, exactly one 60s retry, and opt-in narrow auto-repair that only terminates stale `SkyComputerUseClient ... mcp` children when `computerUse.autoRepair` is true\n- opt-in periodic per-shared-client Computer Use health probes when `computerUse.healthCheckEnabled` is true, using the configured 30/60/120/240 minute cadence and the same live-probe path\n- config/schema/docs for `liveTestTimeoutMs`, `toolCallTimeoutMs`, `leaseTimeoutMs`, `healthCheckEnabled`, `healthCheckIntervalMinutes`, `pluginCacheMode`, `strictReadiness`, and `autoRepair`\n- a window-scope Computer Use lease manager with renewal/preemption semantics for callers that can supply a stable window id\n- `openclaw doctor` surfacing for enabled Codex Computer Use, including configured health cadence and the status command to inspect installation/exposure/live-test layers\n\n## Context\n\nThis addresses the failure mode where one OpenClaw agent could keep using a stale per-agent Codex plugin cache after the desktop app had a newer bundled Computer Use plugin, and where status/setup could report only coarse plugin availability instead of separating install state, MCP exposure, and live runtime health.\n\nThe previous direction in #96730 improved app-server command selection/startup behavior, but it did not make Computer Use readiness globally inspectable across agents, reconcile isolated per-agent plugin caches with the locally installed desktop bundle, or prove the MCP bridge with a real `list_apps` call before reporting readiness. This PR closes those gaps locally and offline; it does not depend on a remote marketplace.\n\n## Notes\n\nThe new lease manager is window-scoped by design, not app-scoped and not host-global. Native Codex MCP notifications are observational, so full pre-execution enforcement requires Computer Use tool payloads to expose a stable window identifier before the MCP call begins. The PR documents that boundary and adds the tested lease primitive for call sites that can provide that scope.\n\nFor local marketplace installation, Codex expects the marketplace **root directory** as the `marketplace/add` source. The individual Computer Use `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` is a plugin descriptor, not a marketplace source. After registration, Codex returns the marketplace manifest path and OpenClaw uses that manifest path plus `pluginName: \"computer-use\"` for `plugin/read` and `plugin/install`.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact head: `5ea5328f30f0d8682136500c6a538893f8d1735d`, based on `origin/main` `f8efe1463930412dfbc78810775fc476b580de10`.\n\nExact-head verification:\n\n- focused cache-default/config/readiness/command tests: 4 files / 282 tests passed\n- Codex extension production and test typechecks passed\n- touched-file `oxfmt --check` passed\n- docs MDX and docs-map validation, plugin-manifest JSON parsing, and `git diff --check` passed\n- isolated current-head ChatGPT.app proof passed: Computer Use installed/enabled, 10 MCP tools exposed, and `list_apps` passed on the first attempt\n\nThe final policy change is intentionally narrow: `pluginCacheMode` now defaults to `independent`; operators must explicitly choose `shared` to allow OpenClaw to reconcile per-agent Computer Use caches. `strictReadiness` remains false by default to preserve startup compatibility while status still reports failed live probes as not ready.\n\nCurrent ChatGPT desktop live proof, executed through the exact PR head in a temporary isolated `CODEX_HOME`:\n\n- selected managed command: `/Applications/ChatGPT.app/Contents/Resources/codex`\n- app-server version: `0.144.0-alpha.4`\n- selected bundled marketplace root: `/Applications/ChatGPT.app/Contents/Resources/plugins/openai-bundled`\n- returned marketplace manifest: `/Applications/ChatGPT.app/Contents/Resources/plugins/openai-bundled/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json`\n- installation ready: true\n- plugin installed/enabled: true/true\n- MCP exposure: 10 tools\n- live `list_apps` probe: passed on the first attempt\n\nLive marketplace-source semantics were also checked with isolated Codex homes:\n\n- the ChatGPT bundled marketplace root directory is accepted by `codex plugin marketplace add`\n- the marketplace JSON file is rejected as an add source because it is a file\n- the individual Computer Use `.codex-plugin/plugin.json` is rejected for the same reason\n\nCompatibility coverage proves:\n\n- `ChatGPT.app` is preferred when both desktop bundle candidates exist\n- legacy `Codex.app` is selected when `ChatGPT.app` is absent\n- plugin-local/package managed binary fallback remains intact\n- bundled marketplace auto-registration and shared-cache reconciliation use the same current-then-legacy precedence\n- explicit app-server command and marketplace overrides remain authoritative\n- explicit marketplace cache namespaces are preserved by shared-cache reconciliation\n- the direct MCP probe sandbox bypass remains constrained to the configured Computer Use server's `list_apps` call\n\n",
        "branch": "fix/computer-use-shared-health",
